# Copy to .env.local and fill in. Only VITE_-prefixed values reach the browser
# (SPEC §10).
#
# ── Two backends, until Phase F ─────────────────────────────────────────────
# P9 moved identity, decks, cards and reviews to Cognito + API Gateway + RDS.
# /progress and card generation still read Supabase, because Phase B rewrites
# generation anyway and porting the progress aggregate is Phase F's work — doing
# either now would mean building it twice. See docs/plans/P9-aws-slice.md.
# So BOTH sets below are required to boot.


# ── AWS (P9) ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# All three come from `cdk deploy` outputs. None is a secret: a pool id and a
# public app client id are in every browser bundle that talks to Cognito, by
# design, and the API endpoint is useless without a valid token.

# The ApiUrl output of SynapseDeck-Api-dev.
VITE_API_URL=https://xxxxxxxxxx.execute-api.us-east-1.amazonaws.com

# The UserPoolId and UserPoolClientId outputs of SynapseDeck-Auth-dev.
VITE_COGNITO_USER_POOL_ID=us-east-1_xxxxxxxxx
VITE_COGNITO_CLIENT_ID=xxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx

# There is NO app client secret, and there must never be one here. A browser SPA
# cannot hold a secret — shipping it in a bundle is publishing it — so the client
# is created with `generateSecret: false` (ADR 0007, infra/lib/auth-stack.ts).


# ── Supabase (until Phase F) ────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Values come from Supabase -> Settings -> API Keys.
#
# This project uses Supabase's modern key system (sb_publishable_… / sb_secret_…),
# not the legacy anon/service_role JWTs — those are deprecated at the end of 2026.

VITE_SUPABASE_URL=https://your-project-ref.supabase.co

# The PUBLISHABLE key. Safe to ship to the browser: it carries no privileges of its
# own, and every request still runs as the `anon` or `authenticated` Postgres role
# under RLS.
VITE_SUPABASE_PUBLISHABLE_KEY=sb_publishable_xxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx

# NOT HERE, EVER: the secret key (sb_secret_…). It maps to `service_role`, which
# holds BYPASSRLS — one line of it in a client bundle exposes every user's data to
# anyone who reads your JavaScript. src/lib/env-schema.ts refuses to start if it
# finds one — that file holds the check; env.ts only applies it.
# v1 does not use a secret key at all; the Edge Function acts as the calling user.

# The generation provider key is likewise not set here. It lives only as an Edge
# Function secret, so it never reaches the client:
#   supabase secrets set GROQ_API_KEY=...



# ── AWS (P8 onward) ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Nothing here is a client secret and nothing here reaches the browser in P8 —
# the frontend does not talk to AWS until P9. These are for CDK, locally.
#
# Credentials are NOT here and never are: the CLI reads ~/.aws/credentials, and
# CI assumes a role via GitHub OIDC with no stored key at all (ADR/D8).

# Budget and alarm notifications. Passed to CDK as -c alertEmail=… or read from
# here. Deliberately not committed with a real value: a personal address in a
# public repo is a spam magnet.
ALERT_EMAIL=you@example.com


# ── The demo sprint's portable spine (DS1, extended at DS2) ─────────────────
# Five seams decide which implementation runs -- four from DS1, plus
# EMBEDDING_PROVIDER from DS2. None has a default, and that is the same rule for
# the same reason: a seam that falls back silently is a deployment writing data
# where nothing will look for it, or generating placeholder cards nobody noticed
# were placeholders.
#
# When AWS returns, four of these five lines are the migration
# (DEMO-SPRINT-BRIEF §8). **EMBEDDING_PROVIDER is the exception** and it is the
# one that is not a config change: see its note below.

# Which model writes the cards. `groq` today; `bedrock` when model access is
# granted; `stub` produces cards that say in their own text that they are fake.
#
# It also decides whether grounded chat works (DS2): answering is a separate,
# narrower interface that only a real provider implements. With `stub` the chat
# endpoint says it is unavailable rather than answering, because a deployment
# with no model has nothing honest to say about someone's documents.
CARD_PROVIDER=groq

# Which vendor embeds the chunks for grounded chat. Fifth seam, added at DS2,
# and the only one with NO offline implementation -- deliberately.
#
# **There is no `stub` and there must never be one.** A stub embedder returns
# vectors whose neighbours are meaningless, so retrieval silently returns
# arbitrary passages and the answer is grounded in noise while looking perfect.
# Unlike a stub card, nothing on screen says it is fake. The resolver refuses
# the value by name.
#
# It is also the one seam that is NOT a configuration change on a populated
# corpus: two models embed into different vector spaces, so switching this
# requires re-embedding every chunk. See services/api/src/lib/embeddings/types.ts.
EMBEDDING_PROVIDER=openai

# Where job state lives. `postgres` for the demo, `dynamo` for AWS. Both
# implementations are kept -- neither is legacy.
JOB_STORE=postgres

# What runs the fan-out. `local` is a bounded in-process loop; `sfn` is Step
# Functions. See services/api/src/data/pipeline-local.ts for what `local` gives
# up: durability across a crash, and per-chunk retry declared rather than coded.
PIPELINE_RUNNER=local

# How many chunks the local runner generates at once. Default 2, and the number
# is a property of your model account's tier rather than of this machine: Groq's
# free tier limits TOKENS per minute (8,000 on the account DS1 ran against) and
# one chunk costs ~1,800 of them. Three at once lost two chunks of four to 429s.
# PIPELINE_CONCURRENCY=2

# Where an uploaded document goes. `s3` presigns a PUT the browser sends
# directly; `local` writes to UPLOAD_DIR on this machine.
UPLOAD_STORE=s3

# Only read when UPLOAD_STORE=local. Deliberately outside the repository: an
# upload directory inside a git checkout is user content one `git add -A` away
# from being committed.
# UPLOAD_DIR=../synapsedeck-uploads


# ── Groq ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# A server-side secret. NOT VITE_-prefixed, so it cannot reach the browser --
# a provider key in a bundle is a published key.
#
# It has existed as a Supabase Edge Function secret since 2026-08-12; this is
# the same key reaching the API instead. DS5 moves it to a secret store.
GROQ_API_KEY=gsk_xxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx

# Which Groq model. Named here rather than hardcoded so a swap is a config
# change; the provider refuses to start without it, for CARD_PROVIDER's reason.
#
# **That refusal earned itself on the first run.** DS1 was written against
# `llama-3.3-70b-versatile` and Groq answered 404 `model_not_found` -- the Llama
# chat models are no longer in this account's catalogue. Because the id is
# configuration rather than a hardcoded default, the fix was this line. Check
# what your key can actually reach before assuming an id is current:
#
#   curl -s https://api.groq.com/openai/v1/models #     -H "authorization: Bearer $GROQ_API_KEY" | grep '"id"'
GROQ_MODEL=openai/gpt-oss-120b


# ── Postgres: Neon for the demo, RDS when it returns ────────────────────────
# The standard PG* variables, which is the whole of D2: `pg` reads them, psql
# reads them, and services/api/migrations/run.mjs reads them. NOTHING IN THE
# CODE KNOWS WHICH POSTGRES IT IS TALKING TO -- that is what makes RDS's return
# a connection string rather than a migration.
#
# Neon's dashboard gives these as a connection string; split it out rather than
# setting DATABASE_URL, because that is not what `pg` reads here.
#
# SSL: db.ts and run.mjs both require it unless the host is localhost, so Neon
# works with no further configuration.
PGHOST=ep-xxxx-xxxx.us-east-1.aws.neon.tech
PGPORT=5432
PGDATABASE=synapsedeck
PGUSER=neondb_owner
PGPASSWORD=xxxxxxxxxxxx

# ── The embedding vendor (DS2) ──────────────────────────────────────────────
# A second vendor, because **Groq has no embedding model at all** -- the
# account's catalogue is chat, audio and prompt-guard only. So the completion
# and the embedding go to different places with separate rate limits, and Groq's
# 8,000 TPM stays a card-generation budget.
#
# A server-side secret. NOT VITE_-prefixed: a provider key in a bundle is a
# published key.
OPENAI_API_KEY=sk-xxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx

# Which embedding model. No default, for GROQ_MODEL's reason and one more: the
# model determines the vector width, and `chunk_embeddings.embedding` is
# `vector(1536)`. A model at a different dimension fails at STARTUP with a
# sentence naming both numbers, rather than at query time with a Postgres type
# error that reads like the database is broken.
#
# text-embedding-3-small is 1536 and costs cents for a demo corpus.
# text-embedding-3-large is 3072 and would need a new migration, not just this
# line -- and a re-embedding of everything already stored.
EMBEDDING_MODEL=text-embedding-3-small


# Local Postgres instead: leave the PG* block unset and set this. dev-api.mjs
# bridges it to PGPASSWORD and defaults the rest to localhost, so a local
# database stays one variable away and neither path is privileged in the code.
# LOCAL_PGPASSWORD=xxxxxxxx
